  "content": {
    "id": "p43-q28-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "28",
    "prompt": "Four Year 12 students want to organise a graduation party.\n\nAll four students have the same probability, $P(F)$, of being available next Friday.\n\nAll four students have the same probability, $P(S)$, of being available next Saturday.\n\nIt is given that $P(F)=\\frac{3}{10}$, $P(S\\mid F)=\\frac{1}{3}$, and $P(F\\mid S)=\\frac{1}{8}$.\n\nKim is one of the four students.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p43-q28-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Is Kim's availability next Friday independent from his availability next Saturday? Justify your answer.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No, since $P(F)=\\frac{3}{10}\\ne\\frac{1}{8}=P(F\\mid S)$.",
          "worked": "For independence, $P(F)=P(F\\mid S)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(F)&=\\frac{3}{10}\\\\P(F\\mid S)&=\\frac{1}{8}\\end{align*}$$\n\nThese probabilities are unequal, so Kim's Friday and Saturday availability are not independent.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p43-q28-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that the probability that Kim is available next Saturday is $\\frac{4}{5}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(S\\cap F)=\\frac{1}{3}\\times\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{1}{10}$, so $P(S)=\\frac{\\frac{1}{10}}{\\frac{1}{8}}=\\frac{4}{5}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}P(S\\mid F)&=\\frac{P(S\\cap F)}{P(F)}\\\\\\frac{1}{3}&=\\frac{P(S\\cap F)}{3/10}\\\\P(S\\cap F)&=\\frac{1}{10}\\\\P(F\\mid S)&=\\frac{P(S\\cap F)}{P(S)}\\\\\\frac{1}{8}&=\\frac{1/10}{P(S)}\\\\P(S)&=\\frac{1}{10}\\times8\\\\P(S)&=\\frac{4}{5}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p43-q28-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Assume that the four students' availability on Saturday is independent. What is the probability that at least one of the four students is NOT available next Saturday?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{369}{625}$",
          "worked": "The students' Saturday availability is independent, so the probability that all four are available is $\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^4$.\n\nThe event “at least one is not available” is the complement of “all four are available”.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}p&=1-\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^4\\\\p&=1-\\frac{256}{625}\\\\p&=\\frac{369}{625}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      }
    ]
  },
